      ]
    }
  ],
  "rules": {
    "Local.0003": {
      "id": "Local.0003",
      "description": "Your application has local or network IO operations.\n\nIf you are targeting Managed Instance on Azure App Service, move persistent I/O to storage mounts (Azure Files/UNC). Keep local writes to temp only; connect to external dependencies via private endpoints/VNet to reduce latency.\n\nIO operations outside of the application folder might not be available on Azure App Service. Review and ensure IO APIs are attempting to access paths that will be available when your app is running on App Service sandbox. If you choose the containerized hosting option, your scenario still might be broken or have scalability issues.\n\nPossible solutions:\n    1. Files that the application needs can be deployed with the application or put in Azure Storage. You can use Azure Storage File Shares as a drop-in replacement for on-premises file share.\n    2. Other services that the application depends on might need to be migrated to Azure, this way you will get the benefit of lower latency in the cloud.\n    3. If your application needs to depend on services that run on-premise, you can connect your Azure and local networks with ExpressRoute, Hybrid Connections, or VPN solutions.",
      "label": "Local or network IO operations detected",
      "severity": "Potential",
      "effort": 3,
      "links": [
        {
          "title": "[Managed Instance] Storage mounts",
          "url": "https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?linkid=2346952"
        },
        {
          "title": "Azure file share",
          "url": "https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?LinkID=2242591"
        },
        {
          "title": "ExpressRoute",
          "url": "https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?LinkID=2250577"
        },
        {
          "title": "Hybrid Connections",
          "url": "https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?LinkID=2242588"
        },
        {
          "title": "Connect an on-prem network",
          "url": "https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?LinkID=2242591"
        },
        {
          "title": "Design to scale out",
          "url": "https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?linkid=2251007"
        }
      ]
    },
    "Perf.0001": {
      "id": "Perf.0001",
      "description": "Synchronous API usage detected.\n\nUsing synchronous APIs can lead to more performance issues in Azure compared to on-premises environments due to different latency profiles. Consider adopting asynchronous code patterns.\n\nIn heavily loaded systems, especially where the I/O is substantial, using asynchronous patterns can improve overall throughput and performance. Asynchronous APIs enable a small pool of threads to manage thousands of concurrent requests without waiting on blocking calls. Instead of waiting for a long-running synchronous task to finish, the thread can handle another request. Conversely, numerous synchronous blocking calls can cause thread pool starvation and degrade response times.\n\nRefer to the links below to learn more about asynchronous programming.",
      "label": "Synchronous API usage detected",
      "severity": "Optional",
      "effort": 1,
      "links": [
        {
          "title": "Asynchronous programming",
          "url": "https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?linkid=2294824"
        },
        {
          "title": "ASP.NET Core Best Practices: avoid blocking calls",
          "url": "https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?linkid=2294727"
        }
      ]
    },
    "Local.0007": {
      "id": "Local.0007",
      "description": "Local OS environment access detected.\n\nIf you are targeting Managed Instance on Azure App Service, Read config via App Settings or App Configuration; avoid machine\u2011specific environment APIs. Persist environment setup via install scripts when needed.\n\nWhen running in Azure App Service environment, your application can read environment variables that you define in App Service settings. Using environment API other than reading environment variables might indicate that your application depends on some local machine\u0027s features and might be broken on App Service sandbox or not scale well. Review your scenarios and either refactor them to be more cloud friendly or choose the option in App Service to host your application in the sandbox for Windows Container Web Apps.\n\nIf you choose to use other Azure hosting options such as Azure Container Apps (ACA) or Azure Kubernetes Service (AKS), environment APIs might work, however scaling still might be an issue.",
      "label": "Local OS environment access detected",
      "severity": "Potential",
      "effort": 1,
      "links": [
        {
          "title": "General App Service sandbox restrictions",
          "url": "https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?linkid=2250496"
        },
        {
          "title": "App Service sandbox for Windows Container Web Apps",
          "url": "https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?linkid=2251963"
        },
        {
          "title": "Design to scale out",
          "url": "https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?linkid=2251007"
        }
      ]
    },
    "Security.0103": {
      "id": "Security.0103",
      "description": "Certificate management packages or configurations detected. This may indicate the application is using local or custom certificate handling, which could require adjustments when migrating to Azure. Consider using Azure Key Vault Certificates for secure storage, lifecycle management, and integration with Azure services.",
      "label": "Certificate management dependency detected",
      "severity": "Potential",
      "effort": 5,
      "links": [
        {
          "title": "About Azure Key Vault certificates",
          "url": "https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?linkid=2335741"
        }
      ]
    },
    "Local.0009": {
      "id": "Local.0009",
      "description": "Native API calls detected.\n\nIf you are targeting Managed Instance on Azure App Service, it\u0027s fully supported there.\n\nThe AppService sandbox generally aims to restrict access to shared OS components. While some Windows native system API might work on AppService sandbox or in containers, many of them are blocked.\n\nIt is also important to avoid native Windows calls if you aim to use AppService or containers based on Linux OS in the future.",
      "label": "Native API calls detected",
      "severity": "Optional",
      "effort": 3,
      "links": [
        {
          "title": "General App Service sandbox restrictions",
          "url": "https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?linkid=2250496"
        }
      ]
    },
    "Local.0012": {
      "id": "Local.0012",
      "description": "Local process start detected.\n\nWhile it is possible to launch new worker processes in either Azure App Service or Azure Kubernetes Service environments, it is an anti-pattern to do so. Both expect to have a single process running and launching others can make it difficult to manage and debug the applications. Expecting another process or service to already be running in parallel with the service will not work in Azure App Service and is an anti-pattern in Kubernetes.",
      "label": "Local process start detected",
      "severity": "Optional",
      "effort": 5,
      "links": [
        {
          "url": "https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?LinkID=2202641"
        }
      ]
    },
    "HTTP.0001": {
      "id": "HTTP.0001",
      "description": "Your application is trying to reach external resources via HTTP protocol.\n\nFor Managed Instance on App Service, secure connectivity to internal/on-prem HTTP services is managed via plan-level VNet integration and private endpoints. Keep inbound traffic restricted to 80/443; capture any required HTTP config in install scripts. Use Bastion JIT RDP only for diagnostics\u2014persist all changes via install scripts. Outbound traffic does not impose similar restrictions.\n\nWhile if targeting other Azure compute services, ensure the resources it is trying to reach are accessible from the Azure environment. If those HTTP requests are sent to internal services or services hosted on-prem, you probably will need to migrate those services to Azure as well.\n\nAlso, keep in mind that Azure App Service supports port 80 for HTTP and port 443 for HTTPS traffic. If you are using any other ports, you need to update them before moving to Azure.",
      "label": "Access to external resources via HTTP is detected",
      "severity": "Potential",
      "effort": 3,
      "links": [
        {
          "title": "[Managed Instance] Bastion JIT RDP",
          "url": "https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?linkid=2346953"
        },
        {
          "title": "[Managed Instance] Install scripts",
          "url": "https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?linkid=2347059"
        },
        {
          "title": "Azure App Service networking features",
          "url": "https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?LinkID=2243264"
        }
      ]
    },
    "Local.0015": {
      "id": "Local.0015",
      "description": "Writing to Event Tracing for Windows (ETW) detected.\n\nIf you are targeting Managed Instance on App Service, forward telemetry to Application Insights/Azure Monitor; avoid reliance on ETW. Use LogMonitor only for temporary forwarding during migration.\n\nWhile if targeting other Azure compute services, writing diagnostics to the ETW is an antipattern for Azure-hosted services. Although some tools (like LogMonitor, etc.) can be used to forward ETW events to the console, there are much better logging options, like Azure Monitor and Application Insights, that will be more productive.",
      "label": "Writing to Event Tracing for Windows (ETW) detected",
      "severity": "Optional",
      "effort": 3,
      "links": [
        {
          "title": "Azure App Service monitoring",
          "url": "https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?linkid=2250851"
        },
        {
          "title": "Application Insights overview",
          "url": "https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?linkid=2250852"
        },
        {
          "title": "Capture logs with App Service Diagnostics training",
          "url": "https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?linkid=2250853"
        },
        {
          "title": "LogMonitor",
          "url": "https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?LinkID=2251308"
        }
      ]
    },
    "Security.0004": {
      "id": "Security.0004",
      "description": "Explicit password-based credentials usage is detected.\n\nEnsure that the dependencies accessed with the credentials and the authentication mechanism used will both work from the target Azure environment.\n\nAlso, consider replacing explicit credential usage with passwordless alternatives. When credentials are loaded from secure sources it might be OK, however it is considered the best practice for Azure applications to use password-less authentication (via managed identity for example) or to store secrets and passwords in Azure App Configuration or Azure KeyVault.",
      "label": "Explicit password-based credentials usage is detected",
      "severity": "Potential",
      "effort": 3,
      "links": [
        {
          "title": "Use managed identities for Azure App Service",
          "url": "https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?linkid=2250759"
        }
      ]
    },
    "Local.0021": {
      "id": "Local.0021",
      "description": "Dynamic assembly loading has been detected.\n\nMake sure the loaded assembly is accessible in the cloud environment.\n\nNote that incidents are not detected in dynamically loaded assemblies.",
      "label": "Detected loading of dynamic assemblies.",
      "severity": "Potential",
      "effort": 3
    },
    "Scale.0004": {
      "id": "Scale.0004",
      "description": "Potential duplex communication is detected.\n\nWebSockets work in most Azure environments and will not block migration. However, they need to be carefully reviewed to ensure the application will work correctly when it is scaled out.\n\nApplications may require a communication backplane if broadcasting a message from one scaled out instance should also broadcast that message from other instances. Similarly, it may be necessary to enable session affinity if it\u2019s required that users reconnect to the same server after a disconnect.\n\nSimilar issues could arise when server sent event streams are used (response content type \u0022text/event-stream\u0022).",
      "label": "Potential duplex communication is detected",
      "severity": "Optional",
      "effort": 3,
      "links": [
        {
          "title": "Design to scale out",
          "url": "https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?linkid=2250798"
        },
        {
          "title": "What is Azure SignalR Service?",
          "url": "https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?linkid=2251300"
        },
        {
          "title": "What is Azure Service Bus?",
          "url": "https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?linkid=2251301"
        }
      ]
    },
    "Security.0001": {
      "id": "Security.0001",
      "description": "MD5/SHA1 usage detected. \n\n Due to collision problems with MD5/SHA1, Microsoft recommends SHA256 or SHA512. \n\n Consider using the SHA256 class or the SHA512 class instead of the MD5 class. Use MD5 only for compatibility with legacy applications and data.",
      "label": "MD5/SHA1 usage detected",
      "severity": "Optional",
      "effort": 1,
      "links": [
        {
          "title": "SHA256",
          "url": "https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?LinkID=2250387"
        },
        {
          "title": "SHA512",
          "url": "https://go.microsoft.com/fwlink/?LinkID=2250388"
        }
      ]
    }
  }
}